ll hire somebody for hr and they'll  worry about ethics something like that  uh  and  finally i we the other place where we  see ethical solutions sort of attempting  to be applied is  uh  in response to maybe a specific issue or  problem  uh you know if if a company has has  managed to have a huge data leak or  something like that uh almost certainly  they will commit themselves uh to better  practices  and commit themselves to being ethical  with people's data and respecting  privacy and you will hear them say  things like we take your privacy very  seriously  um  yeah sure  or or or similarly maybe issues of  transparency  clearly if there is something involving  diversity equity and inclusion  a company or organization has  discriminated against  its members or against someone else or  something like that they will commit  themselves to having  more ethical policies around diversity  equity and inclusion and and respecting  that and they will always say this is  not who we are and this is just kind of  a footnote from somebody who's been  watching these things for many years now  uh when an organization says this is not  who we are  uh  what that almost always means is  unfortunately this is who we are but  we're hoping we can talk you out of  paying attention to it  so um you know those things are all  there and those things have not  in my in my mind and again i would i  would say probably in most people's  minds those things have not addressed uh  some of the ethical questions that that  we would hope they would uh  and and in fact the ethical questions  remain  and and these efforts quite often end up  doing you know pretty much nothing  and that  for me is kind of an interesting  question right  uh why is that  um  and um  it's  it's you know always interesting when  you have this bunch of bright people who  can solve problems and there's this  obvious thing and for some reason it  just can't seem to get done why is that  and there are a number of reasons for  this  there's one though that i don't think  gets gets very much attention and that's  the one that i want to focus on  and that is  the way that we  view our systems in the way that we  approach problems and problem solving  uh so um  we tend to  i if we have any exposure to science or  engineering or things like that we tend  to take a reductionist view of our  systems  and that is  you know viewing a system as a  collection of parts and if you can  understand the parts and then understand  the parts of the parts and the parts of  the parts of the parts and so on all the  way down you can understand the system  uh and this is of course um something  that that that got its start uh  centuries ago uh particularly with with  the two guys that are behind me right  now uh descartes and and newton and then  of course many others this is the way  that we think and this is a way of  thinking that is very successful in a  large number of situations i mean  honestly this sort of reductionist  thinking is why  we are here why we have the  telecommunications in order to have this  conference remotely why we have this  mound of data and and our profession and  all of these things uh comes in large  part to the uh to this approach that was  pioneered those centuries ago  uh the the problem is of course  that it does not always work for all  systems  uh  but if we do have that kind of system  where basically it is the sum of its  parts it's fairly linear one stage leads  to another stage leads to another stage  then if you have a problem  you can solve it by finding the broken  part and fixing it right you know if if  a wing falls off an airplane  presumably you can find where the wing  was attached the airplane you can look  at all the parts find the broken bolt  whatever it might be  and you know what happened and how to  fix it  and you know this leads to  what what some complex systems people  refer to as as the broken part fallacy  or broken part thinking um my my  favorite example i think of this though  in in our business is uh unit tests unit  tests are a classic example of  reductionist thinking uh if we if we  have tests on all of the parts  uh then clearly we shouldn't have a big  problem with the system right because  all of the parts work  uh and we've tested them and we know  they all work and in fact i i don't know  of anybody who writes non-trivial  systems is using software that will  believe that because we know that's not  exactly the whole story  um so  what what is the other part of the story  then what is the the other or at least  one other way of looking at at this  and that would be to think in terms of  of complex systems  and um again i'm not going to complain  claim to to be an an expert uh on the  theory of complex systems there are  people have devoted their careers to  this i've read a couple books so it's  not but in general  a complex system is one where there are  too many parts  and too many possible states uh  for for us to comprehend all of the  possibilities uh in effect they are so  complex that  they're difficult to model and the model  is you know the same size as the system  it's very hard to abstract out one or  two bits to model  and you know i  weather for example is a good example  the classic example of a complex system  uh and within complex systems the the  components  sort of do their own thing they act  locally based on their constraints and  and and and and the inputs locally and  then you know whatever effects they  generate get passed on to other parts  and other parts but there's not a  straight linear progression where if you  start at point a you go through b c d  etc and come out at the other end there  is a complex interaction of things  and that means  with a complex system you can get  emergent behaviors uh they're they're  very hard to pin down as to exactly  where one stage starts and another one  ends  and  that also means that they tend to have  uh non-linear responses to changes in  input uh you know so that a very small  change in some initial condition or some  input can end up having a  a huge effect  somewhere else  uh  and that may or may not be close to  where the initial change was  so this is uh kind of uh you know the  inspiration for the the metaphor of the  butterfly effect  uh that sort of thing uh and it can also  be true with the complex system  that a failure whatever that might be of  a complex system  may or may not be connected to a failing  part  uh it may be a bunch of different parts  failing it may actually be that no part  really actually quote failed but the  interaction of all of those parts uh  ended up producing something that we did  not anticipate that falls into the  category of a failure  so  um that i would argue  is where we're at with  the vast majority of if not virtually  all  of the systems we use processing data  our data the systems that we use to  process our data are almost always  complex systems  it is impossible to know exactly what  every line of our code does in every  possible situation  back when i was doing c in the early 90s  i know  somebody argued that the way that you  could write  bug-free code in c  which is in itself kind of a funny  process funny idea rather if you've  actually tried to write code and see  was to use an interactive debugger and  step through every single line of your  code and every possible branch every  possible state of your code to confirm  that it worked  uh okay even if it might for example be  possible to do that with say a 20 line  function  uh you know you're keeping track of your  uh mccabe uh cyclomatic complexity so  you don't have too many different  branches and all of that you might  actually do that  once you release that 20 line function  into the wild you have no idea  how it's going to interact with the data  that's fed to it and and and you know  what will happen once it returns its  data if it does  so  that i think in my that's certainly my  feeling after after some 30 years of  coding is that in general our software  systems are complex systems  and then  you know we continue from there to  take the the data the decisions what  however you might call it made by a  system like this and feed it into  another system like that which will  again produce a result that gets fed  into yet another system like that and  again none of them know for example  right for you know much for sure about  uh  where the data comes from what's the  condition of the of the data going in  nor do they have any any notion any  control of what will happen with that  data once it goes out  so they're very much components in a  complex system  and i think we can add to that then the  the additional factor of humans doing um  whatever it is they're going to do for  another set of complex complex and  uh often hard to predict reasons  so  that i think  sort of adds up to  a problem in  kind of um  managing this stuff and saying well why  don't we just make our stuff ethical  what's the problem  the problem is  the whole thing is a collection of  complex systems that are working  together and you know it's it's just  sort of all the way down  and that means that  our typical reductionist thinking the  way that we have been trained to think  about uh solving a problem will not help  us unravel this ball of confusion  okay fixing ethics as a component won't  work you can't just find the broken  ethics component  and replace it or fix it  that that does not that's not how it  works  uh  and so when we're thinking about our  systems  i  we we really need to think a little bit  more globally i think it also means that  we can't just have ethics in one part  of our system one part of our  organization without having  an ethical approach everywhere  so  in analyzing and thinking about our  systems and and what we want to do with  them uh we need to think much more  globally we need to think rather than  going down and into the components we  need to kind of go up and out to the the  surrounding context and beyond  so that means we need to consider the  connections with what we're doing with  what's going on in the in the rest of  the world or at least in the rest of our  context we need to consider broader  implications and above all we need to if  we want to behave ethically which i hope  we do  uh we need to have ethics be an integral  part of of the overall systems whether  that's uh you know just  you know a system or our organizations  or our approach to work uh all of that  uh  it also points out that  there is  another way  this brings up another way in which uh  diversity is important  and  i certainly have argued for diversity on  using a number of reasons this is one  that i have not actually used before but  i find it pretty compelling  and that is um  experts  in uh in in complex systems uh  have found that a complex system that is  then optimized  to to reduce its diversity and  redundancy will be much more prone to  catastrophic failure  so that means within our systems if we  want them to be resilient they need to  have a certain amount of of diversity  whatever that might mean  but  in order for that to happen that means  we need to have diversity in the people  creating designing  and guiding those systems  not only  will having a diverse team get you know  help us in terms of creativity uh you  know it's it's kind of been  pretty much known for 60 years that if  you're trying to handle complex problems  the more diverse your team is the more  likely you are to be successful in doing  that  but it's also to to create systems that  themselves have diverse approaches to  things and  this means not just  having  a bunch of different people in the room  it's not just head count  this means that you had they have to  have an impact on the outcome if you  have a hundred very diverse people in  the room but the same one person makes  the decisions all the time based on  their experience that's that's not  meaningful diversity in this sense  so that means that we need to really  practice what we have been talking about  for some time in terms of diversity  equity and inclusion  and that also requires that our  organizations our way of of doing  business our way of dealing with  everybody  has to have an ethical base if not  we will not have  those people so  that that also i think argues for that  and i think finally  another argument for  um  that that this brings up for having a a  an ethical system in place is just that  given the complexity of our systems and  the complexity of our organizations  we we are in situations where there are  many uh decision points where you know  the span of control is is ambiguous uh  where it may not be possible to have a  complete top-down sort of thing  so  we need to have  throughout  sort of a system of ethics in place for  people so that all of those little  decisions along the way  can fit into that ethical framework and  we have a better chance of having an  outcome that that is in some way ethical  so  what does that leave us to do  if you accept any of that um  well  so for one thing we need to increase our  our diversity of people uh in many areas  there's been progress on that in other  areas there's been disappointingly  little  so we need to increase that and it needs  to be again a meaningful inclusion of  people uh in in terms of creating the  final decision not just a head count  we need to as we design and implement  everything we need to continue to  try to force ourselves to think  uh more up and out more beyond the  system more into its context and wider  implications and we need to  uh have some way  to make uh  the the asking of ethical questions  uh and the paying attention to ethical  questions uh an integral part of our  processes of our organizations of of of  how we do things  um so  yeah again i said i had mostly questions  mostly problems those those are kind of  the ones that come to mind  so  yeah you know how are we going to do  that  um  i do have a couple of suggestions  um i don't i don't think i would say  that they're anywhere near being things  that that are our answers  but i do think that we need to be aware  of uh the broader  context as we we go through handling  data and our processes we need to ask  ourselves do we need this piece of data  how are we going to use this piece of  data well this data or with this system  or the data that this system produces  in some way be used to to harm people  will it possibly have a negative effect  uh if  we manage to to use our system to i  don't know uh corner some little bit of  our economy uh and profit uh will that  end up having a devastating effect  somewhere else in the broader system in  the world will we end up uh perhaps  destroying someone's livelihood and in  another part of this complex system that  we inhabit  um we need to in other words make it a  habit to ask ourselves about the wider  impacts and context of what we're doing  um another thing that i think we should  be more aware of is that we can uh at  least take the work that has already  been begun on this topic uh so um there  are  a few projects that have gone on about  uh ethics in data  there are a couple of things the  european commission on ai has produced a  code of ethics relating to  to data and ai  there's the oxford munich code of  conduct relating to to uh  data scientists those are a couple of  places that maybe as we're looking  for  how it is we want to frame our ethics  we can  we can consider those things certainly  we need to promote those projects and  other projects  and i would say participate and shape  them if we can  um  we can possibly  consider  um in in the open source world if we're  releasing software things like that uh  other licensing options uh there is the  ethical source license and other related  uh licenses that attempt to  control the use of something in an  ethical way  um  i i hesitate to think of those as a  final answer uh they strike me as being  an answer to one part of the problem uh  i think i also have a little bit of  concern that uh we have not yet had a  body of ethic of a legal precedent  to to support the effectiveness of that  approach  so uh something that may be worth being  considered but it's still in its early  stages  and finally  of course we can vote with our feet  uh  we in in this business uh have an  enormous amount of leverage right now  collectively  uh people with the skills  that we have those of us in this  conference in our business uh are  increasingly in demand again the pile of  data is is continuing to expand uh  businesses organizations  whoever uh want to want to do something  with that data  and i can tell you  that if  companies need to have and follow  codes of ethics in order to hire people  they will do it uh i've been in various  management discussions where it's like  what do we have to do well if we have to  do this hire people we'll do it so if it  happens to be ethics uh i think  companies will collectively say okay we  can be ethical if we have to i guess uh  if it's a condition for getting the  people that they need  so  we we have  in in that sense the power to insist  that  the companies the organizations the  communities that we are part of uh  follow some sort of code of ethics um we  still have to determine what that is and  and and we still have to follow through  with that leverage to make that happen  but it is something that we can do  so  i guess to wrap this up um  what i'm saying is that um  as humans we actually have  this ability to see beyond  uh just one system and we do have the  ability to look a little bit further and  understand a wider context wider  implications this is something that the  butterfly and the butterfly effect does  not have of course  we can actually learn a little bit and  find out about what  our systems might do and we can think  about what implications they might have  uh we will almost certainly not be right  100 of the time  but i think that we do have that ability  to to look beyond uh and then consider  our actions in the light of something  beyond just uh the the local conditions  the local system our local self-interest  so uh that i would argue is why we need  to to to employ ethics that is the role  i think of of ethics uh  in in this ball of confusion not only as  individuals but businesses organizations  communities uh that ability to to look  beyond and act in a great in the  interest of something greater uh and and  follow an ethical framework to do that  uh is in my opinion  the only way that that we can deal with  this ball of confusion or as the  temptation said you can run run run but  you just can't hide so uh thank you very  much for listening to all of this um  and thank you for having me speak thank  you so much for being with us naomi  thank you so much  i have one question maybe and then we  can close because well you've said many  insightful things one of them that it's  called my attention is like that ethic  seems to be an afterthought  uh  and my question on that is what do you  think we can do to change that that it  starts people start to think that on the  beginning of the process and then not in  the end when things went wrong for  example  i i think that comes down to us as  professionals and what we are willing to  tolerate  um  people  will in general do the things that they  value  so  what what does this mean then you know  if we're willing to to let that pass  then it will continue to be that way i i  think you know in i've seen things  having watched the say the python  community deal with the question of  inclusion and diversity there's still  much work to do but where the python  community was  and you know in attendance at  conferences and in general the inclusion  of people  even 10 years ago compared to now  has been an enormous change because in  fact  the majority of members of the community  would not put up with it not changing  that is what we need to do  perfect  okay so  naomi i thank you so much for your time  and for being here with us  thanks everyone in the audience  thanks so much being here at pi data  global 2021 thank you so much  thank you
 

